# Line endings.
#
# Everything committed here has always been LF, but nothing enforced it. A
# clone with core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows default) checks files out
# as CRLF, and any client that then adds a file without normalising puts CRLF
# into the repository. That is how commit 334840a landed: nine config files
# uploaded through GitHub's web UI, byte-identical to their counterparts on the
# working branch apart from line endings, which made next.config.ts diff as 114
# insertions and 114 deletions and would have conflicted on every line at merge.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on the way in and checks out LF everywhere, so
# the same file has one representation regardless of who committed it or on
# which platform. Note this governs git clients only -- it cannot stop a web
# upload from storing whatever bytes it is handed, so the real safeguard
# remains pushing from a clone rather than uploading through the browser.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never normalise, never diff as text. The two WebP photographs
# are the output of a lossy re-encode and the PNGs are client screenshots --
# a stray byte in any of them is corruption, not a whitespace change.
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff2 binary

# Playwright's visual baselines are compared byte-for-byte. These have not been
# generated yet (CI's first run bootstraps them -- see tests/README.md), so this
# rule is here before the files are, not after something has mangled them.
tests/**/*.png binary

# Generated. Collapsed in reviews so a lockfile refresh does not bury the diff.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
